//? Title: Dijkstra’s Algorithm: Settled Vertices and Stale Heap Entries
//? Subtitle: Actual relaxation traces, the original six-city network and measured lazy-heap bounds
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a directed network with nonnegative travel costs and a starting vertex,
we want the cheapest cost and a route to every reachable destination.
Choosing a cheap immediate edge alone cannot identify the cheapest complete
route. Dijkstra’s algorithm instead settles the smallest accumulated cost
on the current frontier. A three-vertex example shows how improving a
route leaves a stale heap entry without expanding a vertex twice. We retain
the original six-city network and reconstruct its route of cost eleven.
Actual heap removals and edge inspections expose the implementation’s tie
rules and work. Zero-cost cycles, unreachable vertices, invalid weights,
overflow and malformed predecessor records test its boundaries. Sparse
bands and parallel-arc workloads distinguish the familiar simple-graph
complexity from the larger heap possible in a multigraph.
)

\toc

> Start with three vertices and one improved route

Let source 0 have a direct edge to target 1 of cost four and an edge to
vertex 2 of cost one. The edge 2→1 costs one. After expanding the source,
the heap contains tentative routes (1,2) and (4,1). Removing (1,2) discovers
a cheaper route to target 1, of cost two, and pushes (2,1). We then settle
1 at cost two. Finally the old (4,1) entry is removed and skipped: its key
four is larger than the current distance two.

A vertex is settled once, while its id can occur in several heap removals.
The trace below comes from the same kernel as ordinary dijkstra(), with
no replay of prefixes and no report-local shortest-path implementation.
Exactly seven events are recorded: four removals plus three edge inspections.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dijkstra_three_vertices.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(3,{{0,1,4},{0,2,1},{2,1,1}},0,7);
  std::print("Distances: {}; settled order: {}; route to target: {}.\n\n",t.result.distance,t.settled_order,dm::algorithms::reconstruct_path(t.result,1));
  std::print("| removal | vertex | stored key | current distance | stale | heap before | heap after expansion | inspected edges |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.pops.size();++i){const auto& e=t.pops[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,e.vertex,e.key,e.live_distance,e.stale,e.heap_before,e.heap_after,e.relaxation_count);}
  std::print("\nActual pushes {}; pops {}; fresh settlements {}; stale removals {}; peak heap {}.\n",t.work.heap_pushes,t.work.heap_pops,t.work.settled_vertices,t.work.stale_pops,t.work.max_heap_size);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.5;box.height=1.5;box.font_size=24;
  d.node("s",{0,0},std::format("Source 0\nfinal cost {:.0f}",t.result.distance[0]),box)
   .node("t",{8,0},std::format("Target 1\nfinal cost {:.0f}",t.result.distance[1]),box)
   .node("v",{4,-3.7},std::format("Via vertex 2\nfinal cost {:.0f}",t.result.distance[2]),box);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=3.5;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grey;grey.color={148,163,184};grey.width=2;
  d.edge("s","t","cost 4: old tentative route",grey).edge("s","v","cost 1",blue).edge("v","t","cost 1",blue)
   .text({4,2},"A cheaper route leaves an old entry in the heap",25)
   .text({4,-5.3},"Blue route 0 → 2 → 1 costs 2; the later key 4 for vertex 1 is stale",21)
   .save_svg("build/dijkstra_three_vertices.svg");
}
```
