\caption(The blue two-edge route improves the target from cost four to two. The actual removal table shows the later stale entry and its zero outgoing inspections.)
\alttext(A triangle links source zero, via vertex two and target one. The direct cost-four arrow is grey, while the two cost-one arrows are blue.)

> Why settling the nearest frontier is safe

For vertices 0,...,V−1, let delta(s,v) be the least total edge cost of a
source-to-v path, or positive infinity when no such path exists. Store
d[s]=0 and all other tentative distances as infinity. Relaxing an arc
(u,v,w) compares candidate=d[u]+w with d[v]; a strict improvement changes
the distance and parent and pushes the new (candidate,v) heap entry.
Tentative finite labels always correspond to discovered routes, so they
are upper bounds on the true optimum in exact arithmetic.

Suppose the minimum fresh entry u had a shorter true route. On that route,
let y be the first vertex outside the settled set and x its settled
predecessor. Expanding x already gave y a tentative cost at most the
cost of the route prefix. Nonnegative remaining costs make this at most
the full route cost, which was assumed smaller than d[u]. Then y would
be a smaller frontier entry, contradicting the choice of u. Thus a fresh
minimum has its final distance. Strict improvements and nonnegative
weights ensure a settled vertex never needs a new expansion. Zero weights
are valid; strict comparison prevents equal-cost cycles from changing parents.

The implemented frontier is a binary min-heap ordered by (distance,vertex).
Smaller vertex ids break equal-key ties. Outgoing arcs preserve input order,
and equal-cost candidates retain the first predecessor. A later strict
improvement adds a new entry; the older one remains until it is removed.
The stale test is stored_key > current_distance. There is no decrease-key,
no full queue snapshot and no early target stop: the single-source API
drains the heap and returns all reachable distances.

[Dijkstra’s original 1959 paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01386390)
constructs routes in increasing distance order and stops at a requested
destination; it also discusses directed branch lengths. This implementation
uses that settling principle for a complete source traversal with a lazy heap.
The floating-point implementation compares rounded double additions, so the
exact-arithmetic proof does not promise exact-real costs for arbitrary doubles.

> The original six-city road network

Keep the ten original directed roads and source A, with the original
left-to-right city arrangement. Positions serve only to draw the network;
weights are arbitrary travel
costs and are not inferred from Euclidean distances. This educational graph
has no external dataset. An undirected road would require a reverse arc.

The expected distances are A=0, B=3, C=2, D=8, E=9 and F=11.
The cheapest route to F is A→C→B→D→F, costing 2+1+5+3=11.
The alternative through E costs 2+1+6+4=13. Blue arrows in the diagram
are the actual selected original arcs, identified by predecessor_edge in
the trace; vertex parents alone would not identify a chosen parallel arc.
The printed table exposes all ten outgoing-edge inspections, including
candidates that do not improve a destination.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dijkstra_six_cities.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::tuple<std::size_t,std::size_t,double>> roads{{0,1,4},{0,2,2},{2,1,1},{1,3,5},{2,3,8},{2,4,10},{1,4,6},{3,5,3},{4,5,4},{3,4,2}};
  const std::vector<std::string> name{"A","B","C","D","E","F"};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(6,roads,0,19);
  std::print("| city | distance from A | predecessor | original tree arc |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<6;++v){const auto parent=t.result.predecessor[v];
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {} | {} |\n",name[v],t.result.distance[v],parent==dm::algorithms::kNoPredecessor?"root":name[parent],parent==dm::algorithms::kNoPredecessor?"none":std::to_string(t.predecessor_edge[v]));}
  std::print("\nRoute to F: ");for(auto v:dm::algorithms::reconstruct_path(t.result,5))std::print("{} ",name[v]);
  std::print("; cost {:.0f}. Settled ids: {}.\n\n",t.result.distance[5],t.settled_order);
  std::print("| original arc | road | tail distance | prior destination | candidate | improved | heap after inspection |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& e:t.relaxations)std::print("| {} | {} to {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {} | {} |\n",e.edge_index,name[e.from],name[e.to],e.from_distance,e.distance_before,e.candidate,e.improved,e.heap_after);
  std::print("\nWork: {} inspections, {} improvements, {} removals, {} stale, peak heap {}.\n",t.work.edges_examined,t.work.successful_relaxations,t.work.heap_pops,t.work.stale_pops,t.work.max_heap_size);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{0,2},{4,3},{4,0},{9,3},{9,0},{13,2}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(70,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=1.3;node.height=1.3;node.circle=true;node.font_size=24;
  for(std::size_t v=0;v<6;++v)diagram.node(name[v],position[v],std::format("{}\n{:.0f}",name[v],t.result.distance[v]),node);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<roads.size();++i){const auto [u,v,w]=roads[i];dm::graphics::DiagramStroke style;
    const bool chosen=t.predecessor_edge[v]==i;style.color=chosen?dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::graphics::RGB{148,163,184};style.width=chosen?3.5:1.8;
    diagram.edge(name[u],name[v],(i==4 || i==6)?"":std::format("{:.0f}",w),style);}
  diagram.text({5.15,1.08},"8",20,{100,116,139}).text({7.85,1.08},"6",20,{37,99,235});
  diagram.text({6.5,5},"Original six-city network; node labels show city and final cost",25)
   .text({6.5,-2},"Blue arrows: the selected shortest-path tree from A; grey arrows: other roads",21)
   .save_svg("build/dijkstra_six_cities.svg");
}
