> Two vertices can still produce a large lazy heap

Take m parallel arcs 0→1 with descending weights m,m−1,...,1.
Expanding source 0 improves vertex 1 once per arc. Before vertex 1 can be
removed, the heap therefore contains m entries, even though V=2. The
first removal settles vertex 1 at cost one; the other m−1 are stale.
The exact counts are m inspections, m improvements, m+1 pushes/removals,
a peak heap of m and two settled vertices for positive m. This proves
that a lazy heap is not generally bounded by the number of vertices.
It does not alone prove a lower bound on comparator work. The figure uses
base-two logarithms of the positive counts to distinguish heap growth
from the constant two settled vertices; the table retains exact integers.

The canonical workload generator checks the complete edge count before
allocation. Integer weights stay exact through count 2^53; an optional
one-million-edge output cap defaults on. The trace separately budgets
actual events as pop records plus inspected-edge records. Its default is
one million events, and zero disables only that optional cap. Budgets are
checked before the next operation, so the exact m-arc history needs 2m+1
events. Result arrays, adjacency and settled-order storage are outside
that history count. An exhausted budget raises length_error with no partial
trace returned. Ordinary dijkstra() allocates no trace histories.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dijkstra_parallel_arcs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");std::vector<double> arcs,peak,settled;
  std::print("| parallel arcs | inspections | pushes / pops | settled | stale | peak heap | history events | chosen original arc |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t m:{1u,8u,64u,512u,4096u,32768u}){
    const auto edges=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_parallel_workload(m);const auto t=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(2,edges,0,2*m+1);
    if(t.work.edges_examined!=m || t.work.heap_pushes!=m+1 || t.work.heap_pops!=m+1 || t.work.settled_vertices!=2 || t.work.stale_pops!=m-1 || t.work.max_heap_size!=m || t.predecessor_edge[1]!=m-1)return 1;
    arcs.push_back(std::log2(m));peak.push_back(std::log2(t.work.max_heap_size));settled.push_back(std::log2(t.work.settled_vertices));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",m,t.work.edges_examined,t.work.heap_pops,t.work.settled_vertices,t.work.stale_pops,t.work.max_heap_size,t.pops.size()+t.relaxations.size(),t.predecessor_edge[1]);
  }
  const auto edges=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_parallel_workload(8);std::size_t rejected=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(2,edges,0,16);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(2,edges,0,17),uncapped=dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(2,edges,0,0);
  if(rejected!=1 || exact.result.distance!=uncapped.result.distance)return 1;
  std::print("\nEight-arc budget: 16 events rejected {}; 17 succeeds; zero disables the optional cap. Empty graph on two vertices settles {} vertex.\n",rejected,dm::algorithms::dijkstra_trace(2,{},0).work.settled_vertices);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(arcs,peak,"peak heap",{37,99,235}).points(arcs,peak,"",{37,99,235},5).line(arcs,settled,"settled vertices",{220,38,38}).title("Two vertices, many parallel arcs").x_label("log2(parallel arc count)").y_label("log2(actual count)").y_limits(-.5,19).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dijkstra_parallel_arcs.svg");
}
```
