\caption(Base-two logarithms of actual counts show the growing heap peak beside the constant two settled vertices. The table also verifies that stale removals equal the arc count minus one.)
\alttext(A rising blue line for heap entries and a horizontal red line for two settled vertices are plotted in base-two logarithmic coordinates; exact counts remain in the table.)

> What was verified and what remains a convention

Native tests enumerate all 4096 three-vertex directed graphs whose six
possible non-loop arcs are absent or have costs zero, one or two. For every
source, independent Floyd–Warshall distances match both ordinary and traced
Dijkstra: 12,288 source traversals. A multiset-based event certificate checks
every recorded minimum removal, original arc, prior distance/parent,
candidate, strict-improvement outcome and resulting heap size. The original
six-city graph, zero cycles, duplicate arcs and tie order receive explicit
checks. The existing 500 seeded Bellman–Ford comparisons remain in the suite.

Closed-form band and parallel-arc families check final distances, selected
arc identities and work conservation. Exact/one-short event caps, complete
input validation, overflow priority, finite maximum/subnormal costs,
negative zero, malformed predecessor chains and owned trace copies cover
boundary behavior. A malformed public record can no longer force unchecked
parent indexing or an infinite reconstruction loop.

The original finite distances, predecessor conventions, public function
signatures, two-field result layout and formatting remain available. The
new trace distinguishes discovery, settlement and obsolete entries; it does
not conflate nine removals with six settled cities. Nonnegative finite costs
make greedy settlement valid, while explicit floating-point and history
limits keep failures visible rather than presenting them as unreachable routes.

> References

- E. W. Dijkstra (1959), “A note on two problems in connexion with graphs”,
  Numerische Mathematik 1, 269–271.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01386390),
  [open scan](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/JavaAndDS/shortestPath/1959dijkstra.pdf).
